WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.410
Hello and happy new year, this is my first
video for 2020 and i've had a bit of a crazy

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:07.410
week already.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.170
On New Years Eve I got stranded in Brisbane
actually after having some flight troubles

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:16.860
but luckily I was able to make friends with
a couple of strangers I met in the airport

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.700
who were in the same situation, and we were
able to even watch the fireworks together

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.760
in the hotel that the airline put us all up
in.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.640
It's been a bit of a crazy week (month) in
Australia with heaps of bushfires and it's

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.589
super smokey outside which has been really
quite challenging.

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:40.879
I even went to go and try to buy an air purifier
at the shops but the entire town had sold

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:46.629
out of air purifiers as well as smoke masks
so I had to get the next best thing which

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:53.609
was the three last little pots of basil that
were at Aldi and I was thinking that maybe

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:58.390
at least they can give me a little bit of
oxygen, I know that they probably wont, but

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.420
that's why this hilariously sad pot of basil
is by my side today.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:09.630
So it's been nearly a year since I uploaded
a video called I Quit My PhD and it's been

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:16.009
actually a bit over a year since I did quit
my PhD and I thought I would provide a bit

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:21.729
of an update about what i've been up to since
then and the things I've done since leaving

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:26.689
and I guess just answer a few of the major
questions that I've received after posting

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:27.689
that video.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:33.090
I will say I received quite a few comments
on that video or even messages from people

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:39.119
saying that it's given them some kind of inspiration
to want to leave their own PhDs, which is

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.420
not the intention of the video at all and
I want people of course to make their own

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.590
decisions and PhDs (doing one) can be definitely
the right decision sometimes and really good

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:56.299
for you so I don't ever want the purpose of
that video to make it seem like i'm attacking

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:58.710
the PhD system and saying that it's bad.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:04.179
It was just a video that I made because I
had to tell you what I was up to and give

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:05.179
you an update.

00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:10.310
I had to let you know that I had decided to
leave and it was the best decision for me

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.310
to make.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.750
So first of all, do I regret leaving my PhD?

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.650
Well the answer is no, I don't regret it.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:23.740
And i've done lots of cool things in the year
that i've just been kind of out on my own,

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.210
and i'll go through some of those ups and
downs in this video.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:33.000
I don't regret it because even though there
have been ups and downs in this last year,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.570
I've had an enormous amount of freedom.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:41.460
The freedom to do what I want and to work
on what I want and work when I want and I

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:47.390
think that freedom in working is really important
to me and I think that i'm very lucky to have

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.390
it.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:53.760
Of course i'm only able to have it because
of my amazing audience and the support from

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.800
all of my viewers.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.240
So thank you for making this last year possible
for me and making it possible for me to, I

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.400
guess, follow my dreams.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.520
So i wrote down some of my highlights to tell
you about over the last year.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.330
And they include things like travelling to
Zurich where I filmed a few videos, I travelled

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:17.700
to VidCon in the US which was amazing, I went
to VidCon in Melbourne as well.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.720
I gave some talks including at the University
of Sydney and to a group of astronomy students

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.550
in Brisbane and I did lots of awesome collaborations
with other people like my friend Petr, and

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:36.180
Jess, and Cody from Codyslab, and Physics
Girl, Andrew Dotson, I was in the Flammable

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:43.370
Maths recent video and I was even interviewed
on the radio in Australia about being a YouTuber.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.350
And what was awesome about that was that Hank
Green joined us live on the conversation and

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:52.640
that felt like really a highlight to me because
I really look up to Hank.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.990
You might have seen me sharing lately on social
media that I was involved with this Vsauce3

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:03.740
collaboration, I was in the Jumanji episode
of Could You Survive The Movies? and that

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.460
was also quite a big highlight of the year.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.840
And I just wanted to quickly tell you the
story of how that came about because the whole

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.579
thing was crazy and random and I think it's
a good story.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:27.550
So I was at VidCon in the US in July this
(last) year and I just went to one of the

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:29.180
Vsauce presentations.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.960
It was Vsauce 1, 2, 3 on the stage in the
expo hall with quite a big audience.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.980
They were just talking about themselves and
maybe answering some questions and it was

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.370
the first event of VidCon, the first event
that I went to.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.990
And I was a bit late going to the talk because
i'd been at registration.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:54.720
I walked in about half an hour late to the
talk and I'm trying to walk in the back without

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.440
bringing too much attention to myself and
just minding my own business but I heard at

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:06.820
that time, Michael from Vsauce1 was actually
talking about some smaller educational creators

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.990
who he had met the day before, one of which
was me and he did mention my channel name

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.060
as I was walking in.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.590
He didn't know I was there but when I heard
my name I gave him a little wave from the

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:22.330
audience, I wasn't sure if he'd see me or
not but he did and he said 'Oh speaking of

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.170
Tibees, there she is now walking in'.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:29.430
And so people turned around and looked at
me and I gave a little wave and I was a little

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.430
bit embarrassed but it was nice.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:39.210
And then Jake from Vsauce3 was also on stage
and he called out 'Oh Tibees, are you around

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.660
next week?

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:47.700
And would you like to do a collaboration?'
and I of course was super keen but this was

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.620
communication over I guess hundreds of people
in the audience.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.380
And I couldn't really say anything back because
I didn't have a mic so I just did a little

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.950
thumbs up from the crowd and it was kind of
organised right then and there in front of

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:00.950
everyone.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.230
So I think I was just in the right place at
the right time.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:10.330
Jake was looking for a physics channel to
be part of the video and I was keen and around

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.530
so that's how it happened.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:18.440
I'm really glad it did, doing that video was
an awesome experience, I got to meet Casey

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.800
Neistat and do a scene with him which is something
I never thought would happen and just to see

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.510
what it's like to be on the set of a super
high production video.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:33.580
A highlight that I can't forget to mention
is the whole veggietales saga.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:39.780
I repeatedly apologize to viewers who have
been recommended the veggietales video from

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.000
me.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:48.030
The whole thing with that I guess exploding
from 4 thousand views to 4 million views in

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:52.740
2019 was something that I didn't expect and
it's been kind of funny, it's been kind of

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.060
a meme.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:07:00.390
Lowlights that I will talk about include just
like the nasty comments that I get, the gross

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.370
and obsessive comments as well.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:09.260
There's like some really terrible stuff that
I read that just ruins my faith in YouTube

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:14.770
comments and has kind of made it a place that
I don't really want to read through a lot.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:19.199
Having said that though there are lots of
good comments, probably outweighing the bad

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:25.020
ones ten to one and thank you to the people
who do leave really nice and encouraging comments

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:31.170
because i guess the existence of those is
what enables me to not take these horrible

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.370
ones very seriously.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:37.889
While I was still a PhD student and while
I was starting to grow my YouTube channel,

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:42.690
I guess that what I was doing was going to
the lab and being at uni all day, working

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:49.669
on my PhD all day and then working on my videos
at night and on weekends and really trying

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:53.010
to be efficient and squeezing it into my spare
time.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:59.880
So I was kind of getting both done but I knew
that I was really stretching my time thin.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:05.160
I guess I thought that when I left my PhD
that I would free up these huge chunks of

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:10.160
time during the day that I would be able to
just spend on videos so I would be able to

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.220
do videos all day as well as all night and
maybe with that same intensity but that's

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:17.940
not what I found happened.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:26.380
I guess I left my PhD so i was no longer doing
that but I don't think the amount of time

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:31.389
extra that I was able to spend on videos really
entirely replaced the time that I was spending

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:32.389
on the PhD.

00:08:32.389 --> 00:08:38.419
I think if anything I was still just working
on videos at about the same rate, getting

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.270
them out and uploaded.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.279
Maybe they were slightly higher quality and
I definitely couldn't have done as many if

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:46.079
i'd still been a student.

00:08:46.079 --> 00:08:52.279
But I also think that I increased the amount
of time that I wasted or procrastinated or

00:08:52.279 --> 00:08:56.259
did other things, some of which were good
and some which were probably a waste of time

00:08:56.259 --> 00:09:03.399
so that is something that I kind of dislike
about the last year, I kind of feel like I

00:09:03.399 --> 00:09:07.480
wasted a lot of time, especially because my
expectations were so high.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.680
I thought that if I don't have this PhD to
do at all, i'll be able to spend so much time

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:18.720
on videos but I guess I didn't have the same
intensity of working on it when it was no

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.189
longer a side hobby and when I had all day
to do videos I guess I didn't spend all day

00:09:24.189 --> 00:09:25.189
doing videos.

00:09:25.189 --> 00:09:29.360
But I don't know if that's good or bad but
that's just a thought that i've had.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:35.119
I have been plagued by a bit of anxiety this
year both related to being a YouTuber and

00:09:35.119 --> 00:09:36.709
completely unrelated.

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:42.759
But overall i'm really happy with where I
am and i'm just excited to keep doing the

00:09:42.759 --> 00:09:47.410
same thing and keep doing what i'm doing for
this next year and into the future.

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:51.800
So i'm going to answer now a few more specific
questions.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:57.461
The number one question when I tell people
i'm pursuing being a YouTuber is How to you

00:09:57.461 --> 00:10:02.050
get paid? and so I thought i'd briefly mention
that.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:08.579
The way that i'm able to continue making videos
on this channel is that I make money from

00:10:08.579 --> 00:10:15.509
Adsense so that is directly scaled with views
and it's just directly from YouTube themselves

00:10:15.509 --> 00:10:19.009
from the ads that play before videos or around
videos.

00:10:19.009 --> 00:10:25.480
I generally don't put midrolls in just because
I don't really like midrolls myself and I

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:30.269
feel like if I can avoid putting them in in
most cases then I just will.

00:10:30.269 --> 00:10:36.459
But there's that and there are sponsors as
well, you've probably seen me do a few sponsored

00:10:36.459 --> 00:10:37.459
videos.

00:10:37.459 --> 00:10:43.679
I try not to do too many sponsors like I know
some channels do every video sponsored and

00:10:43.679 --> 00:10:48.970
i'd probably make a lot more money if i accepted
a lot more sponsorships but I try to just

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:54.329
keep it at the minimum that I kind of need
to just keep the channel alive.

00:10:54.329 --> 00:11:01.060
And I do only work with brands that I think
are spreading something good, are good products,

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:06.149
and have the right attitude towards their
customers and genuinely want to support learning

00:11:06.149 --> 00:11:07.730
content on YouTube.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:13.220
I also made a Patreon account and you can
support me there is you want to directly support

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:14.220
the channel.

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:19.800
Another question I got is What does a day
in your life look like? and I kind of find

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.699
this a bit hard to answer because sometimes
i'm not even really sure myself.

00:11:23.699 --> 00:11:30.339
But the most probably average day that I could
give you is that if i'm working on a video

00:11:30.339 --> 00:11:35.660
then working on anything from planning the
video which can take a while, especially if

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:40.499
it contains math or physics ideas that I don't
want to get wrong like a proof or something

00:11:40.499 --> 00:11:43.199
i'll try and like do a bit of reading about
it.

00:11:43.199 --> 00:11:47.639
There's also the time it takes to come up
with a good topic which I think is most of

00:11:47.639 --> 00:11:54.199
the success of the video (determined by) so
i'm always actually thinking about trying

00:11:54.199 --> 00:11:59.029
to come up with good topics and trying to
do a bit or market research I guess you'd

00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:04.290
call it to see how I think they'd do on YouTube
before I make them.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:10.999
I do everything myself in my videos, occasionally
I will have a cameraman if you see the handheld

00:12:10.999 --> 00:12:18.720
shots but other than that it will be me who
does the research, the idea, the filming,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.769
the editing which takes longer than filming
and I always film way more footage than I

00:12:22.769 --> 00:12:29.920
end up using by quite a big factor so i'll
edit that down, uploading it and promoting

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.589
it and trying to engage with the audience
a bit and just generally replying to emails

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:41.560
and doing everything around uploading videos
because there are a surprising amount of random

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.110
tasks that I find come into this job.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:50.769
And if i'm not doing any of that then i'll
be doing some other random thing maybe writing

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:54.929
which is something that I haven't made the
most progress with but will continue to do

00:12:54.929 --> 00:12:57.149
and hopefully I can share with you eventually.

00:12:57.149 --> 00:13:03.100
The next idea I want to cover is What do I
miss about my PhD if anything?

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:09.110
And there are things that I miss, like being
a PhD student is a whole lifestyle you have

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:14.809
your friends and your lab mates and everything
like that and I guess I had to step back from

00:13:14.809 --> 00:13:22.230
all of that which was a bit sad and I do miss
that social aspect of having people to work

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.579
with and having lab mates and people who are
just in it together.

00:13:26.579 --> 00:13:33.800
I do probably get a bit lonely just making
videos by myself but yeah that's one things

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.959
and also just like the facilities that you
get when you're at uni.

00:13:39.959 --> 00:13:45.860
Whether that's literally the labs and like
the microscopes and stuff and just the access

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:51.589
to all the cool stuff int he lab which can
come in useful, I miss that but also things

00:13:51.589 --> 00:13:59.369
like the uni library and the ability to check
out books that would otherwise be really expensive

00:13:59.369 --> 00:14:01.860
but you can get on your student card.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:08.579
So yea there's things I miss but they're not
that big and they're not big enough to want

00:14:08.579 --> 00:14:11.749
to go back to it, i'm really enjoying what
i'm doing.

00:14:11.749 --> 00:14:17.170
And i'd probably miss the freedom of this
a lot more if I was to ever go backwards.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:21.230
So am I thinking about ever going back to
my PhD?

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:23.899
Well the answer is right now, no.

00:14:23.899 --> 00:14:30.550
I'm just not, I think that i'm only still
kind of just getting started with what i'm

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:35.189
doing, i've still got so many ideas I want
to do, i'm still so excited to try so many

00:14:35.189 --> 00:14:42.110
more things and i'm far from running out of
ideas or anything like that.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:49.009
The last thing I want to talk about is the
question, Do I think other PhD students should

00:14:49.009 --> 00:14:50.009
quit?

00:14:50.009 --> 00:14:51.009
And when should they quit?

00:14:51.009 --> 00:14:57.660
Now I could give some kind of fuzzy advice
a long the lines of, it's a shame to be spending

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.829
your time working on something that's not
right for you and if it's not right for you,

00:15:01.829 --> 00:15:03.290
you should leave.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:08.410
But maybe if you are in this situation you
don't know if it's right or wrong for you

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:11.059
and you just don't know if you're on the right
path.

00:15:11.059 --> 00:15:13.840
And of course if you were on the wrong path
you would leave but you don't know if you're

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.410
on the wrong path.

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:22.720
Well I think maybe some more solid advice
i've realised is that you probably know some

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:23.720
of your strengths.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:28.999
And you know what you are good at and you've
had enough experience of things to know what

00:15:28.999 --> 00:15:30.629
your strengths are.

00:15:30.629 --> 00:15:36.769
And hopefully you can tell whether or not
your PhD or your job or whatever program it

00:15:36.769 --> 00:15:41.490
is, is using those strengths and is allowing
you to use those strengths and to combine

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:45.410
those strengths and the skills that you know
you have.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:50.489
And if it's not bringing out the best in you
and the things that you're best at then you

00:15:50.489 --> 00:15:52.920
probably should leave.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:58.129
I guess one of my strengths that I thought
was really important to me was the ability

00:15:58.129 --> 00:16:03.959
to be creative and to combine art and science
and that's what I really loved doing.

00:16:03.959 --> 00:16:09.230
And whilst I was kind of good at the stuff
I was doing in my PhD, it wasn't using my

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.639
best strengths.

00:16:10.639 --> 00:16:16.699
I knew what my strengths were and I wasn't
using them, and now I think i'm using them

00:16:16.699 --> 00:16:17.699
a lot more.

00:16:17.699 --> 00:16:23.139
So maybe that is some more solid advice to
find out if you are on the wrong path or the

00:16:23.139 --> 00:16:27.209
right path and then you can decide what to
do about it.

00:16:27.209 --> 00:16:33.399
So thanks for listening (watching) this video
and thank you to all my viewers and supporters

00:16:33.399 --> 00:16:38.649
for making this last year possible for me
and i'm excited about what we can do together

00:16:38.649 --> 00:16:39.649
this year.

00:16:39.649 --> 00:16:40.149
Thanks :)

